{
  "dom_id": "question-184178",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Prove that ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ is odd if $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}-2n+1 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{3}-2n+1={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ is {{field-7}}.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
